Expands steno entries with count operators (`%d`, `%[...]%]`, `%<N>`) into multiple resolved entries, one per count value, and validates the full corpus round-trip. Test doubles for `show()`, `one()`, and `at()` render chunks and extract entries by count for assertion.

Count expansion replaces the count operator with concrete landings: `%d` becomes a number, `%[sep|body%]` repeats the body across iterations with separator, and `%<N>` resolves to a landing index calculated backward from the total landing pool. Computed landings like `%(d+1)` are evaluated per iteration; `%(2d-1)` going negative is a hard error. An entry with `@count BANK` must have a count operator in its template; having neither or both is an error. The landing pool is built left-to-right; `%<0>` always gets the highest index, `%<1>` the next down, regardless of written order — resolving `%<N>` before enough landings exist errors.

`expand_counts(entry) -> Result<Vec<ExpandedEntry>, E>` expands a single entry with a count annotation; returns one entry per count value. `expand_all(entries) -> Result<Vec<ExpandedEntry>, E>` expands all count entries in a batch. Non-count entries pass through unchanged. The full corpus in `dict.steno` (loaded at test time from `CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR/../../dict.steno`) expands to a larger set with no unresolved operators.